  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Foxy Bolete is a medium-sized fungus in the bolete family, characterized by its reddish-brown coloring that resembles a fox's coat. It is found in New England and other temperate regions, typically fruiting in summer and fall.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Reddish-brown to orange-brown cap with a velvety texture, reminiscent of a fox's fur",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "Yellow pores on the underside that may stain blue when bruised; stem is pale yellow with reddish fibrils near the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flesh Reaction",
      "description": "White flesh that may slowly turn slightly blue when cut or damaged",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "The Foxy Bolete grows in mixed forests with conifers and hardwoods, showing a preference for acidic soils and appearing near birch, oak, and pine trees.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'vulpinum' comes from the Latin word for fox, referring to the mushroom's russet coloring that resembles a fox's coat.",
